{
 "_about": "SERIES SHELF of Ancient Classics for English Readers (Edinburgh and London: William Blackwood and Sons, 1870-1880; American issue Philadelphia: J. B. Lippincott), edited by the Rev. W. Lucas Collins: short English introductions to each Greek and Latin author, with the substance of the works retold and quoted in English translation. Twenty-seven of the twenty-eight volumes, each a first-decade printing dated on its title page (most in Roman numerals): Homer's Iliad and Odyssey, Virgil, Aristophanes, Plautus and Terence, Lucian, Livy, Thucydides and Cicero (W. Lucas Collins), Herodotus (George C. Swayne), Horace (Theodore Martin), Aeschylus (Reginald S. Copleston), Xenophon and Aristotle (Sir Alexander Grant), Sophocles and Plato (Clifton W. Collins), Pliny's Letters (Alfred Church and W. J. Brodribb), Euripides and Tacitus (William Bodham Donne), Juvenal (Edward Walford), Hesiod and Theognis and Catullus, Tibullus and Propertius (James Davies), the Greek Anthology (Lord Neaves), Ovid (Alfred Church), Demosthenes (W. J. Brodribb), Lucretius (W. H. Mallock) and Pindar (F. D. Morice). Cicero is the Project Gutenberg text (PG 11448); the rest are raw Internet Archive OCR, clean 0.89-0.94. Not taken: Anthony Trollope's Commentaries of Caesar (PG 55926), already on the trollope-works shelf. Mallock's Lucretius is kept here with its series rather than on the w-h-mallock shelf. The Aeschylus volume prints its title as Æschylus, so its title check reads weak; author and imprint were read on the scan. Each title gates on its own author's name. US public domain. No uids minted.",
